  mutants; 20 + 3 = 23.)
  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-13 (F2 pass, the kimi CLI seat, which counted table2.out's APPLIED and NEW lines): this read
  "21 of 21 ... 24 mutants + base; 21 + 3 = 24" -- base counted as a mutant.  The SAME miscount F1 corrected in
  table 1's POSTTABLE, repeated in the table written the same afternoon: the parenthesis that claimed to show the
  count was itself the wrong count.  `grep -c '^NEW' table2.out` = 24; minus base = 23. ***
  *** AND THE SCOPE (codex, F2): of the 23, two survivors are EQUIVALENT (dogft, ctlrdft) and one is NOT (acc8mid, a
  real narrowing the rows cannot see, disclosed as clause (e) debt).  So: 21 non-equivalent mutants, 20 detected, 1
  disclosed survivor -- "20 of 20" is true only of the targeted subset the table was built to measure. ***

TABLE-1 INSTRUMENT (40 rows) -- the pass-2 seats' claimed escapes, MEASURED
    addrrc 40/40   mmreads 40/40   fdebug 40/40   wstore 40/40   acc8dog 40/40   acc4low 40/40   annlow 40/40
  All seven escaped the 40-row detector, as claimed (kimi CLI, grok, minimax, codex).  The Opus seat's own escapes
  (a_revw, a_lo10, a_imask, a_istat, a_time, x_mc4, x_mc8, l_addrmask, l_noonce) were measured 40/40 on the same
  instrument in opus_pass2_20260913/logs/ and are not repeated here.  Every escape of the 40-row detector is killed by
  the 71-row one.

THE CHANNEL LINE, checked as the mandate asks: PRETABLE 2 named each mutant's red rows at writing time and all 20 sets
were exact -- including the two that needed a trace through a whole session (wstore's `imask ctl': the refused sb +0x18
stores 0 into INTMASK, so the later accepted lw cannot reassert; acc8dog's four control rows).  Table 1's one miss
(acc8w, a sequential-session trace) was the lesson applied here.

DENOMINATOR, for the closing pass: the refusal cells {1,2} x {R,W}, each by a SINGLE-cell fall-through (only1r, only2r,
only1w, only2w) and jointly (table 1's len1only/len2only/readonly/wronly/fallsw); the switch labels by an address-keyed
fall-through at each observable one (REV both directions: revwrite, table 1's rev rows; CONTROL writes: table 1;
INTSTAT: a_istat; INTMASK: imaskft; TIME: timeft; the default arm: addrrc, mmreads, a_lo10) and at the two equivalent
ones (dogft, ctlrdft); the accept cells at both ends of the window (table 1's six at +0x30; acc8dog, acc4low, x_mc4,
x_mc8 at the far end); a refused store that lands (wstore); the announce's channel (fdebug), text (fmtswap, l_noonce,
l_addrmask, annlow) and latch (nolatch, latchinv); the dispositions (revert, fault, exit0, return1).  NOT swept, and
disclosed: an accept arm narrowed at an INTERIOR slot (acc8mid); two-cell refusal x label joints beyond the rows above.

*** CORRECTION, recorded here and NOT in PRETABLE.out (editing that file would destroy its mtime order proof):
PRETABLE 2's first line says the instrument went "42 -> 50 row() call sites".  Counted with the ast deriver over the
frozen copies: table 1's crime_width_probe.table1.py has 26 call sites and table 2's crime_width_probe.table2.py has
48 -- not 42 and 50 (both were counts of the WORKING file taken mid-edit, and a first draft of THIS correction repeated
the 42).  The emitted counts (40 and 71) are right; only the call-site literals were wrong.  Deriver:
    python -c "import ast,io,sys;t=ast.parse(io.open(sys.argv[1]).read());print(sum(1 for n in ast.walk(t)
               if isinstance(n,ast.Call) and getattr(n.func,'id','')=='row'))" <frozen copy> ***
